00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.990 align:start position:0%
 
benjamin franklin is well known as one  of the founding fathers of america but  there was another side to this renowned  statesman he was a polymath someone who  has in-depth knowledge of numerous  subjects franklin was a writer publisher  scientist and inventor who continually  reinvented himself benjamin franklin was  born in boston massachusetts on january  17 1706 to candlemaker josiah franklin  and his second wife abaya folger he was  their eighth child and final son he also  had seven half siblings from his  father's first marriage at the time of  his birth boston was already a thriving  seaport filled with sailors merchants  preachers and prostitutes growing up  next to the charles river he enjoyed  swimming and early on the inventor in  him tinkered with ways to glide through  the water more quickly he made his own  version of fins his were two wooden hand  paddles when franklin was six his family  moved from a tiny two-bedroom house to a  larger home in the heart of boston this  allowed his father to entertain  interesting dinner guests which benjamin  later remarked helped to improve the  minds of his children as for his formal  education benjamin didn't have much his  father only had enough money to send him  to school for two years squashing the  expectation of his son going to harvard  in the hopes of eventually joining the  clergy so benjamin's formal education  ended when he was just 10 years old  someone wondered whether he would have  been as great of a scientist as sir  isaac newton if he received more of an  education but what franklin lacked in  formal schooling he made up for with his  passion for reading he even stopped  eating meat and became a vegetarian in  part so he could save to buy more books  his love of reading and writing  flourished further when he worked at his  brother james printing shop as an  apprentice he even got a chance to  publish his own articles for his  brother's newspaper one of the first in  america under the pseudonym mrs silence  do good when he was 17 years old  franklin left boston for new york hoping  to break out on his own and find work as  a printer but he didn't have any luck so  he went further south to philadelphia  which was then the largest city in  america he worked in several print shops  around town and later for a philadelphia  merchant by that time benjamin was tall  muscular comfortable with any company he  kept and very charming he boarded at the  reed family home and fell in love with  15 year old deborah reed deborah's  mother refused to allow their marriage  because he didn't have a penny into his  name he continued courting deborah in  years later he entered into a common  common-law marriage and she ended up  raising his illegitimate son william  whom he had with an unknown woman  deborah and benjamin had two other  children a son francis who died of  smallpox at the age of four and a  daughter named sarah franklin spent his  twenties helping to improve the  community in philadelphia he formed the  junto a club consisting of young men  from diverse backgrounds that met on  friday evenings to discuss morals  politics philosophy and science they  discussed questions like have you lately  observed any encroachment on the just  liberties of the people the junto  meetings were modeled off english coffee  houses which franklin knew well having  worked in a printing shop in london for  a time members loved to read and decided  to pool their money to buy books that  everyone could take their turn enjoying  they created america's first lending  library the library company of  philadelphia franklin later became the  publisher of the pennsylvania gazette  which served as a voice of opposition to  british colonial rule not only did he  print the paper he often contributed to  it like this political cartoon depicting  a snake in pieces called join or die  that was meant to rally the american  colonies together each piece of the  snake represented a colony though there  were 13 colonies yet only eight pieces  because he grouped some colonies  together  he also used his newspaper to write  about his inventions including his  famous experiments with electricity with  the help of his son william he attached  a wire to the top of a kite which picked  up an electric charge from thunderstorms  and conducted electricity all the way  down the white kite string this led to  his invention of the lightning rod which  protects houses from lightning when it  carries the electric charge harmlessly  to the ground without burning the house  down franklin's groundbreaking work on  electricity made him famous that name  recognition would come in handy as he  was tasked with convincing the french to  support american independence he spent  several years stationed near paris as  america's first diplomat his popularity  in diplomatic skills apparently paid off  france sent troops and supplies to aid  the colonies during the revolutionary  war and formally recognized the us as a  sovereign nation franklin was appointed  postmaster general during the war and in  that role was responsible for making  sure mail was delivered as quickly as  possible by horseback that was a crucial  line of communication that held the 13  colonies together despite all the hats  he wore franklin somehow found time to  devise inventions throughout his life he  created bifocal glasses which have two  halves the upper for distance the lower  for reading the franklin stove was a  meadowline fireplace that provided more  heat and less smoke than the open  fireplaces used at the time  one of his more unusual inventions was  his beloved glass armonica he worked  with the glassblower to create the  musical instrument inspired by musicians  who passed their fingers around the  brims of glasses filled with water it  was very popular during his time mozart  even composed music for it if inventing  weren't enough he also had a major  influence on the science of demography  or studies of the population he took  notes on population growth and realized  the american population was the fastest  growing on earth and would surpass that  of england in a century his writing on  the subject was not without controversy  in an essay he wrote why increase the  sons of africa by planting them in  america where we have so fear and  opportunity by excluding all blacks and  tawnys of increasing the lovely white  and red but perhaps i am partial to the  complexion of my country for such kind  of partiality is natural to mankind like  many of his time franklin owns slaves  and viewed them as inferior to white  europeans but in his later years he  became a vocal proponent of abolishing  slavery he also didn't hold other groups  in high regard when there was an  increase in german immigrants to america  he feared it would bring down the  country as the population grew franklin  wrote about the importance of improving  farming practices to meet the booming  demand for food when he retired from the  printing business he bought a 300 acre  farm in nearby new jersey it became his  experimental station where he carried  out projects in drainage crop rotation  and fertilization looks like the  founding father was now a founding  foodie he also introduced many  international foods and plants to  america such as swiss barley scotch kale  chinese rhubarb and silk he was exposed  to international goods thanks to the  time he spent in london on diplomatic  missions he made many trips there from  the 1750s to the 1770s but would later  sever ties to support the american  revolution in fact he had a falling out  with his son william who remained loyal  to britain franklin never forgave his  son for quote taking up arms against me  franklin was part of a committee that  drafted the declaration of independence  which explained why the 13 colonies  regarded themselves as independent and  no longer under british rule he's the  only person to sign all three major  documents that established the united  states as an independent nation the  declaration of independence the treaty  of paris and the united states  constitution after the constitution was  ratified in 1788 franklin wrote to a  scientist friend in france and gave us  one of the most famous sayings our new  constitution is now established  everything seems to promise it will be  durable but in this world nothing is  certain except death and taxes  throughout his middle and later years  franklin was obese and suffered many  health problems he had a long history of  lung disease and gout a form of  inflammatory arthritis that results in  red and swollen joints on april 17 1790  franklin died at his home in  philadelphia when an abscess in his  lungs suddenly burst he was 84 years old  it's estimated 20 000 people attended  his funeral nearly the entire population  of philadelphia at the time today every  year tens of thousands of visitors  tossed pennies at franklin's gravestone  for good luck and not to a famous saying  attributed to him a penny saved is a  penny earned though apparently he never  actually said this he did once right  remember that time is money without a  doubt benjamin franklin used his time  wisely if you'd like to hone your skills  just like franklin did and who knows  maybe become a polymath yourself there's  a great and interactive way to learn  brilliant is an amazing tool for  brushing up on your science technology  engineering and math skills it almost  doesn't feel like learning because  you're having fun while solving  interactive problems their everyday math  course helps you see fractions  percentages and geometry in a new light  my personal favorite is the logic course  which helps improve your critical  thinking skills if you ever get stuck  there are hints to help you out and  explanations if you get the incorrect  answer there are no tests on brilliant  and you can work at your own pace to get  started for free visit brilliant.org  newsthink or click the